<section id="about-lorem-ipsum" class="border-b border-hairline bg-canvas py-20 sm:py-28">
	<div data-scroll-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[800px] px-6">
		<p class="mono-eyebrow mb-3 text-mute">The complete guide</p>
		<h2 class="text-heading-lg text-pretty text-ink">
			Built for real workflows
		</h2>

		<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-6 text-body-md text-body">
			<p>
				This tool gives designers, developers, and writers exact control over
				their placeholder text. Instead of pasting the same fixed block of
				Latin from an old bookmark, you configure a fresh output every
				time — choosing the vocabulary, the amount, and the export format before
				you copy a single character.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Generate by paragraph, word, or exact character count
			</h3>
			<p>
				Beyond paragraphs, there's a word mode for short labels and buttons,
				a sentence mode for captions, and a dedicated character-count mode
				for the cases those don't cover. Need an exact length for a meta
				description, a tweet, or a fixed-width form field? Set the unit to
				Characters, enter a number like 500, and the output is trimmed to
				that exact length — precisely 500 characters, not "close enough,"
				which matters when you're testing truncation, line wrapping, or
				database column limits.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				More than Latin: themed word banks, including a funny option
			</h3>
			<p>
				Classic Latin is the default for a reason — its letter and word-length
				distribution reads naturally without distracting reviewers from the
				actual design. But Latin doesn't always match the tone of the product
				you're mocking up, so this tool ships five alternate word banks:
				Corporate jargon for business decks, Tech/Startup vocabulary for
				developer tools, Foodie language for menu and recipe layouts,
				Cat-themed filler for playful projects, and — for when a design
				review needs some levity — a Funny mode built from absurd, humorous
				filler words instead of Latin. It swaps in words like "kerfuffle,"
				"bamboozle," and "flapjack" while keeping the same natural sentence
				rhythm as the Latin original.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Every paragraph, ready to export
			</h3>
			<p>
				Once your text is generated, the live word and character counters
				update instantly so you know exactly what you're pasting. A single
				click copies the result to your clipboard — no manual select-all, no
				extra whitespace. Export as plain text for a copy deck, HTML with
				each paragraph already wrapped in a &lt;p&gt; tag for direct use in
				markup, or Markdown for a README, CMS field, or static site. Because
				everything runs client-side, there's no page reload and no waiting:
				change the theme, the amount, or the unit, and the output regenerates
				live.
			</p>


			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Where placeholder text fits in a design workflow
			</h3>
			<p>
				Placeholder text earns its place at three stages of a project. During
				wireframing, it lets you test hierarchy — headline, subhead, body,
				caption — without anyone debating wording. During visual design, it
				exposes real typographic problems: awkward line breaks, orphaned words,
				rivers of white space, and headings that wrap to three lines on a phone.
				During development, it fills CMS templates, email layouts, and component
				libraries so engineers can check overflow, truncation, and empty states
				before editorial content exists. Using realistic lengths at each stage
				matters more than the exact words: a 60-character title and a 160-character
				description behave very differently from a single short word.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Common use cases
			</h3>
			<ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 pl-5">
				<li><strong>Website and app mockups:</strong> fill hero sections, cards, and article templates so stakeholders review layout rather than copy.</li>
				<li><strong>Typography testing:</strong> compare typefaces, sizes, line heights, and measure (line length) on a realistic block of text.</li>
				<li><strong>Print and editorial layouts:</strong> flow text through columns, pull quotes, and captions in brochures, magazines, and reports.</li>
				<li><strong>Development and QA:</strong> seed databases and test form limits, truncation, pagination, and search results with predictable filler.</li>
				<li><strong>Presentations and demos:</strong> populate slides and product screenshots without exposing confidential or unfinished copy.</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				How this compares to other lorem ipsum generators
			</h3>
			<p>
				Many lorem ipsum generators return a fixed block of Latin and stop
				there. This one differs in a few practical ways. You can generate to an
				exact character count rather than a rough paragraph count, which is
				useful for meta descriptions, social posts, and database fields. You can
				choose English-language themed vocabularies such as Corporate or
				Tech/Startup when Latin feels out of place in a mockup, and you can paste
				your own text to build a custom word bank. Output can be exported as
				plain text, HTML, or Markdown. Everything runs in your browser, so there
				is no sign-up, no usage limit, and nothing you generate is uploaded.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				Tips for using placeholder text well
			</h3>
			<ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 pl-5">
				<li>Match the length of real content. If real headlines will be 8 words, generate 8 words, not 3.</li>
				<li>Test the extremes: try both a very short and a very long value to see how the layout copes.</li>
				<li>Use a themed word bank when the tone matters, for example Foodie for a restaurant menu.</li>
				<li>Do not ship it. Replace placeholder text before launch; search engines and readers treat it as low-value content, and it can hurt a page's usefulness.</li>
				<li>Check accessibility with realistic text: contrast, line length (45 to 75 characters is comfortable), and reflow at 200% zoom.</li>
				<li>Avoid sharing screenshots with Latin filler in client-facing reviews if the audience may mistake it for finished copy; label it clearly instead.</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="text-heading-md text-ink">
				A short history of the filler text
			</h3>
			<p>
				The passage traces to Cicero's <em>de Finibus Bonorum et Malorum</em>
				(45 BC), a Latin work on ethics. Printers in the 16th century scrambled
				sections of it to make type specimen books, and the text reached a wide
				audience in the 1960s when Letraset dry-transfer sheets used it, and
				again in the 1980s and 90s when desktop publishing software bundled it as
				default sample copy. Because the words are scrambled and partly invented,
				the text has no meaning to distract a reader, yet its letter frequencies
				resemble real prose, which is exactly what a layout test needs.
			</p>

			<p>
				Whether you need one word for a button label, a full page of
				paragraphs for a content mockup, or an exact-length string for a
				character-limited field, this tool is built to handle all of it from a
				single form — free, instant, and with no sign-up required. Paragraph,
				sentence, word, and character controls are ready in one place whenever
				a layout needs filler text.
			</p>
